\title{\Large \bf R2CC: Reliable and Resilient Collective Communication for Distributed Learning}

\author{
{\rm Wei Wang, Nengneng Yu, Sixian Xiong, Zaoxing Liu}\\
University of Maryland
}

\maketitle



\begin{abstract}
Modern ML training and inference now span hundreds to tens of thousands of GPUs, where failures can waste 10–15\% of GPU hours due to slow recovery. Common network errors and link fluctuations can cause up to 50\% of job interruptions, triggering timeouts that often terminate entire jobs, forcing expensive checkpoint rollback during training and request reprocessing during inference. We present R2CC, a fault-tolerant communication library that provides lossless, low-overhead failover by exploiting multi-NIC hardware. R2CC performs rapid connection migration, bandwidth-aware load redistribution, and resilient collective algorithms to maintain progress under failures. We evaluate R2CC on four 8-GPU H100 InfiniBand servers and via large-scale ML simulators modeling up to 1024 GPUs with diverse failure patterns. Experiments show that R2CC is highly robust to inter-node network failures, incurring less than 1.1\% training and 3\% inference overhead under active failure scenarios. Compared to existing fault-tolerant approaches, R2CC reduces failure-induced overhead by up to 92\% for training and 98\% for inference. R2CC is open-sourced at \url{https://github.com/r2cc-project/R2CC}.

\end{abstract}

\label{sec:bg-stack}

\noindent\textbf{Hardware stack in GPU Clusters.}
Modern GPU clusters employ a two-tier communication fabric that separates high-bandwidth intra-node communication from constrained inter-node links.\footnote{Examples include production LLM training clusters reported by Meta~\cite{gangidi2024rdma}, Alibaba~\cite{qian2024alibaba}, and others.}
Within a server node, GPUs are connected via NVLink or NVSwitch, providing hundreds of GB/s of bidirectional bandwidth and low latency between devices on the same node.
Between nodes, GPUs communicate through RDMA-capable NICs (e.g., InfiniBand or RoCE) attached to the host over PCIe.
Because a single NIC typically offers substantially less bandwidth than the intra-node fabric, production servers aggregate multiple NIC ``rails'' per node, and the effective inter-node connection between a pair of servers consists of several physical paths that may traverse different switch ports or even different ToR switches.
Recent designs, such as multi-plane fabrics~\cite{qian2024alibaba,oci-multiplanar,khashab2026high} and GPU servers with directly attached NICs, change how NICs connect to switches and GPUs; Appendix~\ref{app:discussion} discusses R2CC under these designs.
GPU buffers are exposed to these NICs via memory registration, allowing NICs to perform direct RDMA reads and writes on GPU memory without CPU-side copies, but only along links for which registration and queue pairs have been established.

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{Common hardware failures.}
Failures in this stack fall into three categories: GPU failures, intra-node link failures, and inter-node network failures. GPU failures include hardware, memory, or other errors that render a device unusable. Intra-node link failures are faults in the GPU fabric within a server. Inter-node network failures are faults that affect the available bandwidth or the set of network paths between two otherwise live GPU endpoints.

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{R2CC scope.}
As a CCL, R2CC targets \textbf{inter-node network failures} that change the available paths or bandwidth between live GPU endpoints, while at least one alternate path remains usable. This includes hard faults such as NIC, port, cable, or ToR-port failures, as well as gray failures, such as link flapping, that surface as transport errors. GPU failures that remove the compute endpoint, including rare cases where network faults co-occur with GPU failures~\cite{Revisiting_Reliability}, are out of scope; they are typically handled by training and inference frameworks. Intra-node link failures are also out of scope because proprietary protocols such as NVLink/NVSwitch lack a CPU/CCL-visible recoverable transport boundary, such as per-transfer completion/error reporting and replay state. Supporting such failures would require redesigning the intra-node transport protocol. Intra-node link failures are also not reported as frequent errors in the hardware stack. Additional discussion is provided in Appendix~\ref{app:failure-scope}.

\label{sec:bg-failures}
Figure~\ref{fig:layer} illustrates the software layering of distributed collective communication.

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{Framework layer.}
The ML framework layer owns the training or inference loop. Systems such as PyTorch~\cite{paszke2019pytorch}, DeepSpeed~\cite{rasley2020deepspeed}, and Megatron~\cite{shoeybi2019megatron} partition work across GPUs and maintain model or request state across iterations. They drive training steps such as forward, backward, and optimizer updates, or serving request steps such as prefilling and decoding~\cite{kwon2023efficient,agrawal2024taming,zhong2024distserve}. The framework delegates cross-GPU data movement to the CCL layer through collective or point-to-point operations.

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{CCL layer.}
The CCL layer implements the collective and point-to-point operations delegated from the framework. Libraries such as NCCL~\cite{NCCL}, RCCL~\cite{RCCL}, and UCCL~\cite{zhou2025extensible} organize the participating GPUs into channels and realize each collective as a ring or tree schedule. In a ring schedule, data is partitioned into chunks that circulate across GPUs, advancing one hop per step. For collectives such as Broadcast, ReduceScatter, or AllGather with input size $D$, each server sends and receives approximately $D$ bytes. AllReduce is commonly implemented as a ReduceScatter followed by an AllGather, resulting in 2D bytes of communication per server.

Each transfer step in CCL follows a specific performance-oriented protocol. In throughput-oriented protocols, bulk data transfer is decoupled from completion notification: the GPU processes data only after the receiver’s CPU observes a corresponding notification. In contrast, low-latency protocols embed both payload and progress metadata within the same transfer, allowing the GPU to consume data incrementally as it arrives. The CCL layer coordinates these transfers and drives communication progress.

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{RDMA verbs layer.}
The RDMA verbs layer provides the interface between the CCL and the network hardware. Inter-node communication is realized through queue pairs (QPs) established between endpoints. The CCL issues work requests (WRs) to initiate communication and polls completion queues (CQs) for work completions (WCs), which indicate progress or errors. Two operations are commonly used here: RDMA Write transfers data directly into a pre-registered remote GPU buffer without involving the receiver's CPU. RDMA Write with Immediate (WriteImm) additionally carries a small inline value, consumes a pre-posted receive request on the remote side, and generates a completion event to notify the receiver. These primitives enable high-throughput, low-overhead communication but provide only limited visibility into higher-level communication semantics.

\label{sec:related}
\noindent\textbf{Framework-level failure handling.} The framework layer handles hardware failures at the granularity of GPU ranks or entire server nodes. Checkpoint-based approaches, such as Gemini~\cite{wang2023gemini} and ByteCheckpoint~\cite{bytecheckpoint}, provide fast in-memory restore, efficient save/load, and resharding across training configurations. Other systems, such as TrainMover~\cite{lao2024trainmover}, use standby machines and live migration to replace failed workers while preserving the training configuration. Oobleck~\cite{oobleck} and ReCycle~\cite{gandhi2024recycle} adapt training execution after worker changes through pipeline templates, replica recovery, or pipeline-schedule adaptation. On the model serving side, D\'{e}j\`{a}Vu~\cite{ICML2024} streams and replicates KV-cache state so that requests can resume on a healthy replica. All of these approaches implicitly assume that the compute endpoint itself is lost or must be removed. As a result, they recover by replacing or excluding entire workers. They are therefore ill-suited for failures where both GPU endpoints remain operational and only a subset of the inter-node network paths is affected.

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{RDMA transport-level failure handling.}
At the RDMA transport layer, failures manifest as queue pair (QP) errors or retry-exhausted operations. For instance, SHIFT~\cite{shift} and LubeRDMA~\cite{luberdma} attempt to mask these failures by transparently reissuing outstanding work requests over alternative paths, allowing transfers to complete without exposing errors to higher layers. However, these mechanisms operate at the level of work requests and completions, without visibility into collective schedules. Under modern low-latency CCL protocols (e.g., LL/LL128 in NCCL~\cite{NCCL}), GPUs may consume data before the corresponding completion is observed by the CPU, which is outside the transport layer's control. As a result, systems like SHIFT~\cite{shift} need to disable low-latency protocols to maintain data correctness at the cost of performance.

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{CCL-layer responses.} At the collective communication layer, mainstream libraries such as NCCL and RCCL have long adopted fail-stop semantics: any transport error aborts the collective, terminates the process, and forces job-level recovery. Although the latest NCCL versions (v2.29.7 and later) support active-active failover across multiple ports or NICs assigned to a GPU, this approach reduces bandwidth as it fails to rebalance collective workloads and schedules. 
Other recent efforts attempt to improve resilience within the CCL layer. For example, AdapCC~\cite{zhao2024adapcc} detects non-responsive workers via timeouts and coordinates with an elastic training controller to continue with a reduced worker set, but it still fails once a fatal error occurs during an ongoing collective. ICCL~\cite{chen2025efficient} pre-establishes primary and backup connections between GPU pairs and migrates traffic upon failure; however, it routes all traffic through a single backup path without adjusting the collective schedule, creating a bottleneck on the affected node.

We observe that \textbf{the key missing piece in today's software stack is a resilient CCL layer.}
Inter-node network failures should be handled within the CCL layer rather than escalating to process or job-level failures. Ideally, the CCL should continue ongoing collectives transparently and correctly, so that collective operations remain indistinguishable from failure-free execution. Beyond merely preserving correctness, it should also adapt communication schedules to the degraded topology and mitigate performance bottlenecks caused by reduced bandwidth. Finally, it should expose degradation signals to upper layers, enabling frameworks to make informed decisions about continuation or recovery, rather than reacting only after a crash occurs.

\label{sec:design-mig}

When network errors occur during active communication, CCLs typically trigger crashes that disrupt the entire ML job. R2CC intercepts these errors before they escalate into fatal crashes. In this section, we discuss R2CC's real-time failure detection and mitigation mechanism that consists of
(1) \textit{bilateral failure awareness} to quickly determine that a problem exists, (2) \textit{precise fault localization} to identify where the failure is, and (3) \textit{live migration} to continue the communication with alternative connections. We also introduce \textit{degradation signaling and restore} to detect recovered paths and restore the original route.

A connection failure may stem from either endpoint, such as faulty NIC hardware, driver issues, or the physical link, but RDMA exposes only coarse transport errors (e.g., retry-exceeded), which lack the granularity to identify the impaired side. Localization therefore focuses on determining whether the failure is \textit{local}, \textit{remote}, or \textit{bilateral} to guide subsequent schedule optimization. 

\textbf{Our approach:}
R2CC introduces dedicated probe QP pools, isolated from data paths, to localize failures. When an error is detected, R2CC performs \textit{three-point triangulation} (for communicators spanning $\ge$3 servers): both endpoints and an auxiliary NIC on a third server of the same communicator issue zero-byte RDMA Writes as probes. A failed NIC produces immediate local probe errors, while its peer observes timeouts; a broken link yields timeouts at both endpoints, with the auxiliary NIC distinguishing single-endpoint vs. dual-endpoint impairment. By correlating probe outcomes, R2CC precisely identifies the fault and broadcasts it to all ranks via the OOB channel. For communicators spanning only two servers, R2CC directly marks both endpoints of the failed path unavailable and resumes on the backup connection. R2CC also periodically reprobes to detect component recovery (e.g., NIC resets, cable fixes), adapting probe frequency and strategy based on observed failure and recovery patterns.

\label{sec:signal-restore}

As R2CC keeps NCCL's collective API unchanged, network degradation is exposed through a separate query, \texttt{r2ccCommGetDegradationState()}, scoped to the communicator. The framework can poll this query at its own control points and chosen frequency, typically between iterations or requests. Each call returns the degradation epoch, the current state, the affected path, and the residual bandwidth, allowing the upper layer to decide whether to reschedule, throttle, or escalate to a heavier-weight recovery.

Restore is off the hot path. After failover, the current collective continues on the backup path while R2CC periodically reprobes the affected path. Once the original path becomes usable again, R2CC revalidates or re-establishes the primary path and switches subsequent collectives back at collective boundaries, so the primary and backup connections never access the buffer concurrently. It then reverts the degraded topology adaptation and updates the degradation signal exposed to the framework.

\label{sec:single-failure}

When a failure is hot repaired, all in-flight messages are migrated from the failed NIC to a backup connection, which must now share bandwidth with other GPUs. If the collective continues to use its original schedule, all traffic formerly on the failed NIC now flows into the backup NIC, effectively doubling its load and significantly slowing communication.

We show that such a performance bottleneck can be \textit{largely} mitigated via a failure-aware design for all collectives: (1) \textit{R2CC-Balance} is a simple but generic load-balancing technique on top of NCCL's existing schedules. It evenly distributes traffic across the remaining healthy NICs and works for all collectives. (2) \textit{R2CC-AllReduce} is a novel AllReduce schedule that further improves throughput under failures.

We implement R2CC as a drop-in extension of 3K lines of C++ code to NCCL 2.23.4, allowing existing ML frameworks to leverage it without any modifications. Our implementation integrates R2CC strategies into the host transport layer and collective scheduling logic, and builds R2CC-AllReduce from standard NCCL collectives on sub-communicators.

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{Transport hooks and backup paths.}
R2CC intercepts NCCL's communicator initialization at the \texttt{ncclNet} plugin layer to establish backup connections alongside the primary connection. We extend the memory registration path to register each GPU buffer with all NICs of the server, and augment the connection setup logic to create additional queue pairs over backup NICs. The resulting handles are organized into a per-channel failover list ordered by PCIe distance to the source GPU, allowing the failure recovery logic to select the topologically closest healthy NIC.

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{Failure detection and live migration.}
R2CC augments the net plugin and proxy send receive progress loops to handle network failures without crashing the application process. The modified InfiniBand plugin monitors Completion Queue (CQ) and Queue Pair (QP) errors, reporting them as failed requests to the proxy. Upon error, the plugin issues a lightweight RDMA probe and exchanges messages via the bootstrap network to confirm the failure location. Once a failure is confirmed, the proxy rolls the channel back to the last chunk with a confirmed completion, purges outstanding work requests, transitions the channel to the backup connection, and reissues the remaining chunks.

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{R2CC-Balance and R2CC-AllReduce.}
R2CC-Balance is integrated into NCCL's enqueue and planning logic. Before NCCL selects an algorithm and instantiates rings or trees, R2CC inspects the health status records of NICs on each node. The planner then redistributes the affected channels across the remaining healthy NICs on the same node. R2CC-AllReduce creates two sub-communicators with \texttt{ncclCommSplit} after repair. Most of the data runs a global AllReduce across all nodes, while the rest runs a Partial AllReduce on the sub-communicator that excludes the degraded node. The degraded node's share of that part is then reduced onto a healthy helper rank and broadcast back in pipelined chunks. These collectives run on separate CUDA streams ordered by events, so the phases overlap without custom kernels. HotRepair, R2CC-Balance, and R2CC-AllReduce all support CUDA Graphs (Appendix~\ref{app:discussion}).

We evaluate R2CC extensively on a real testbed and large-scale simulations. We find that:
\begin{packeditemize}
    \item R2CC achieves less than 1.1\% overhead on training and less than 3\% on inference when failure happens, reducing overhead by up to 92\% compared to training baselines and up to 98\% compared to inference baselines (§\ref{exp:training}, §\ref{exp:inference}).
    \item R2CC demonstrates robust scalability, incurring only 4.3\% overhead with 10 concurrent failures across a simulated 512-GPU cluster (§\ref{exp:training}).
    \item R2CC maintains up to 93\% of baseline throughput for collective operations during failures (§\ref{exp:overhead}).
\end{packeditemize}

\label{exp:overhead}
\label{sec:microbenchmark}

We evaluate R2CC's primitive communication performance using NCCL-tests on four nodes of our physical testbed, measuring communication operations bus bandwidth across message sizes from 8B to 16GB under a failure scenario.

\paragraph{AllReduce Performance.}
Figure~\ref{fig:allreduce-h100} compares four configurations: vanilla NCCL with no failure, R2CC-HotRepair, R2CC-Balance, and R2CC-AllReduce. We also run the full R2CC pipeline with failures injected and fixed to measure R2CC's behavior with failover and restoration.

Without failure, vanilla NCCL achieves up to 393 GB/s for large messages. When a NIC fails (12.5\% total bandwidth loss), the R2CC-HotRepair approach suffers approximately 50\% throughput loss for large messages, as expected from the single-NIC bottleneck on the affected node.

R2CC's two strategies exhibit complementary strengths. For small messages ($<$32MB), R2CC-Balance achieves significantly lower latency, maintaining 88--96\% of normal throughput while R2CC-AllReduce ranges 73--94\% due to its data dependency coordination overhead. For medium messages (32MB--256MB), both strategies perform similarly at 88--96\% of normal throughput. For large messages ($\geq$512MB), R2CC-AllReduce achieves superior performance, around 88--93\% of normal throughput compared to Balance's 86--88\%, by reducing the data volume that the bandwidth-constrained node must transmit. These large-message ratios match the closed-form predictions from Section~\ref{sec:single-failure}: at $n = 4$ and $X = 1/8$, Balance throughput is expected to be $(1-X) = 87.5\%$ of baseline and R2CC-AllReduce throughput $\frac{2(n-1)(1-X)}{2(n-1)(1-X)+nX} = 91.3\%$, both falling within the measured ranges.

In practice, R2CC leverages an $\alpha$-$\beta$ performance model, using machine-specific link latency ($\alpha$) and bandwidth ($\beta$) parameters provided by NCCL along with the operation's data size, to dynamically select the optimal strategy at runtime. This approach ensures that the crossover point adapts to different hardware configurations and message sizes, rather than relying on a fixed threshold.

We evaluate the full R2CC pipeline from HotRepair to restore with a 1~GB message size nccl-tests AllReduce benchmark and compare its performance across different policies. As illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:tiaolaitiaoqu}, we inject a network failure midway through iteration 5 and restore the connection at iteration 11. R2CC immediately repairs the in-flight collectives when the failure occurs, which causes a temporary dip in the average bandwidth for iteration 5. Starting from iteration 6, R2CC adapts to the optimal R2CC-AllReduce strategy, continuing the operation with only a 6.9\% overhead compared to the failure-free baseline. Once the network is restored at iteration 11, R2CC automatically reverts to the original AllReduce schedule. We also evaluate a case where a NIC fails mid-training between two scheduled checkpoints in Appendix~\ref{appendix:checkpoint}.

\paragraph{More results on other collectives.} Beyond AllReduce, we evaluate R2CC-Balance on AllGather, ReduceScatter, and point-to-point (SendRecv) operations. Appendix~\ref{appendix:collectives} shows that R2CC-Balance consistently maintains 85-89\% of normal throughput across all three operations.

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{Collective communication optimization.} Prior work improves distributed ML communication by optimizing or automatically generating collective schedules~\cite{shah2023taccl,liu2024rethinking,devraj2025efficient}, and more recently by co-designing collectives with the underlying topology or fabric~\cite{kumar2025pccl}. These approaches target communication efficiency under normal or statically specified conditions, but do not address how to preserve an in-flight collective or adapt communication online after partial network failures. R2CC instead focuses on CCL-layer resilience under failures, combining online repair with post-failure schedule adaptation.

\smallskip\noindent\textbf{Network diagnostics and monitoring.}
There have been efforts to diagnose network issues in GPU clusters. System-level diagnosis and monitoring frameworks~\cite{liu2023hostping,liu2024hostmesh,liu2024r} isolate bottlenecks and localize likely fault sources using host- and network-level measurements in RDMA/RoCE clusters. A complementary line develops algorithmic telemetry mechanisms, including sketch-based monitoring systems~\cite{liu2016one, zhang2024octosketch}, for general and fine-grained network measurement. These works improve observability and fault localization, but they do not repair an in-flight collective or adapt collective schedules after partial inter-node failures.

\label{app:failure-scope}

Table~\ref{tab:r2cc-failure-scope} summarizes R2CC's failure-handling scope, separating failures R2CC handles in-flight (fully or partially) from out-of-scope fundamentals that are left to higher-layer recovery.

\paragraph{Gray failures and link flapping.}\label{app:gray-failure}
R2CC detects failures through transport-level errors, such as work request completions that exceed the retry limit, and initiates recovery as described in \S\ref{sec:design-mig}. However, link flapping or packet loss masked by RDMA retransmissions does not produce such errors, leaving R2CC unaware of the degradation. Consequently, fault localization and HotRepair are not triggered, and collective operations continue to complete correctly, but at reduced throughput (Table~\ref{tab:r2cc-failure-scope}).
While detecting such gray failures is a limitation of the current design, R2CC-Balance (\S\ref{sec:single-failure}) already distributes traffic in proportion to per-NIC bandwidth. A possible extension would therefore be to use observed per-channel bandwidth to drive rebalancing, potentially mitigating gray failures that manifest as sustained throughput degradation.

\label{app:bridge-based}

Algorithm~\ref{alg:reranking} inserts bridge nodes with broader rail connectivity between ring neighbors whose surviving rail overlap falls below the global bandwidth threshold, repairing only the problematic edges while leaving the rest of the ring intact.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\small
\caption{R2CC Bridge-Based Re-ranking}
\label{alg:reranking}
\textbf{Input:} Logical Ring $R$, Rail Sets $S$ \\
\textbf{Output:} Optimized Ring $R'$
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\STATE \textbf{Initialize} $R' \leftarrow R$, $Candidates \leftarrow \emptyset$
\STATE $B_{global} \leftarrow \min_{n \in R} |S_n|$
\FOR{$i \leftarrow 0$ \textbf{to} $|R| - 1$}
    \STATE $u \leftarrow R[i]$, $v \leftarrow R[(i+1) \pmod{|R|}]$
    \IF{$|S_u \cap S_v| < B_{global}$}
        \STATE $Candidates.\text{add}((u, v))$
    \ENDIF
\ENDFOR
\STATE \textbf{Sort} $Candidates$ by severity (gap size) descending
\FOR{each pair $(u, v)$ in $Candidates$}
    \STATE $BestBridge \leftarrow \text{None}$
    \FOR{each node $w \in R' \setminus \{u, v\}$}
        \STATE $x \leftarrow \text{PrevNode}(w), y \leftarrow \text{NextNode}(w)$
        \STATE $NewCap \leftarrow \min( |S_u \cap S_w|, |S_w \cap S_v| )$
        \STATE $RemovalCap \leftarrow |S_x \cap S_y|$
        \IF{$NewCap \ge B_{global}$ \textbf{and} $RemovalCap \ge B_{global}$}
             \STATE $BestBridge \leftarrow w$
             \STATE \textbf{break}
        \ENDIF
    \ENDFOR
    \IF{$BestBridge \neq \text{None}$}
        \STATE $R'$.Relocate($BestBridge$, between $u, v$)
    \ENDIF
\ENDFOR
\STATE \textbf{return} $R'$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\label{appendix:collectives}

\paragraph{R2CC Inference Performance Under Multiple Failures.}\label{multi-failure-inference} We further evaluate R2CC's resilience under multiple concurrent failures on our two-node testbed. Unlike training workloads that may scale to hundreds of nodes, inference deployments typically use fewer nodes per model instance, reflecting production requirements for latency and cost efficiency. Our multi-failure experiments use vLLM engine to serve a 405B model with TP=8 and PP=2 configuration. We inject varying numbers of failures to reduce available NICs on a single node and feed requests at QPS=0.1, ensuring the system operates within its capacity to measure steady-state overhead. As shown in Figure \ref{fig:multi_failure}, both TTFT and TPOT overheads remain within 0-5\%, exhibiting minimal variance due to system underlying overhead, even as multiple network device failures reduce majority bandwidth. This demonstrates that R2CC fully exploits the ample bandwidth headroom in inference workloads: even under more severe and numerous failures on network devices, the remaining links still suffice to allow the system to continue serving with overhead close to zero.

\paragraph{Other Collective and Point-to-Point Operations.}
Beyond AllReduce, we evaluate R2CC-Balance on AllGather, ReduceScatter, and point-to-point (SendRecv) operations. Figure~\ref{appendix:fig:nccl-test-allreduce} shows that R2CC-Balance consistently maintains 83--90\% of normal throughput across these three operations for large messages, while the R2CC-HotRepair approach suffers approximately 50\% throughput loss due to its single-NIC bottleneck.

These results demonstrate that R2CC's load-balancing mechanism generalizes effectively beyond AllReduce, providing consistent fault tolerance for the full range of communication primitives used in modern distributed ML systems.

\label{app:quality}
\paragraph{Setup.} We train GPT-2 (124M)~\cite{radford2019language} on WikiText-103~\cite{merity2016pointer} with PyTorch DDP for 1,000 updates on three CloudLab servers, each with two V100S GPUs and three NIC ports. After update 400, we install a drop rule on one NIC port of one server for the rest of the run, leaving the other NICs up. Runs are deterministic, and each failure run is paired with a failure-free run of the same seed (three seeds per policy).

\paragraph{Results.} Without failures, R2CC is bit-identical to the upstream NCCL 2.23.4 it forks from: the same losses and the same hashes of reduced gradients and parameters at every checked update. Under the failure, R2CC completes the interrupted AllReduce on the backup connection and continues under R2CC-Balance or R2CC-AllReduce without restart or rollback. HotRepair itself is lossless: the repaired collective at update 401 produces a bit-identical reduced gradient. Differences begin only at update 402, where R2CC-Balance and R2CC-AllReduce redistribute data across the remaining NICs and thereby change the order in which gradients are summed; floating-point addition is not associative, so a different order rounds differently. To verify that reordering is the only effect, we re-sum the recorded gradient inputs in double precision as an exact reference: both NCCL's and R2CC's results stay within a relative error of $5.8\times10^{-8}$ of it, i.e., ordinary FP32 rounding. Figure~\ref{fig:quality} shows the paired trajectories: the per-update loss deviation stays within $2.4\times10^{-3}$ nats/token with no consistent sign, and final test perplexity rises by at most 0.023\% (Table~\ref{tab:quality}).

Since the H100 testbed in \S\ref{sec:setup} is a shared cluster that cannot be held for long, the artifact is evaluated on CloudLab\cite{Duplyakin+:ATC19} r7525 servers. It validates the following claims:
(1) R2CC recovers in-flight collectives from a NIC-port failure quickly, losslessly, and correctly instead of aborting the job (\S\ref{sec:design-mig});
(2) the R2CC-Balance and R2CC-AllReduce scheduling optimizations follow the trends reported in \S\ref{sec:microbenchmark}, and their measured performance is within 1\% of what the model in \S\ref{sec:single-failure} predicts for this testbed;
(3) R2CC is compatible with CUDA Graphs (Appendix~\ref{app:discussion});
(4) training quality is preserved under a NIC failure (Appendix~\ref{app:quality}).
Collective results are checked for correctness on both healthy and failed networks.
